{
  "requirement": "Bedrock configurations MAY supply `credentials` containing nonempty\n`access_key_id`, `secret_access_key`, and optional nonempty `session_token`.\nOllama configurations MAY supply a nonempty `api_key`. Habeas MUST reject a\nBedrock record combining explicit credentials with `aws_profile`, and an Ollama\nrecord combining `api_key` with `api_key_env`.\n\nExplicit credentials MUST be passed directly to the selected provider without\nchanging process environment variables or writing credential files. Saved\nexplicit values MUST round-trip; credentials resolved from profiles or the\nenvironment MUST NOT be serialized back. Debug output, configuration diagnostics,\nusage records, and initial prompts MUST NOT include credential values. TOML\ndiagnostics MUST NOT echo credential-containing source lines. An active workspace\nfile containing explicit credentials MUST be excluded from model source reads by\ndefault under {cite}`requirements.authoring.source_tools`; explicit workspace path\noverrides still apply.\n\nDocumentation MUST discourage literal credentials in workspace files and\nrecommend AWS profiles or environment-variable references. Examples MUST use\nplaceholders and explain that workspace files may be committed or read as source\nwhen explicitly allowed.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge `requirements.authoring.credentials` satisfied. The implementation covers each obligation, it is marked with `{impl}`, and the tests exercise the main cases.\n\n**Validation (accepted and rejected shapes)**\n- `BackendConfig::validate` in `src/config.rs` (lines 399-490) rejects `credentials` combined with `aws_profile`.\n- It rejects `api_key` combined with `api_key_env`.\n- It rejects empty `access_key_id`, `secret_access_key`, `session_token` and `api_key`.\n- `BedrockCredentials` and `OllamaConfig` make `session_token` optional.\n- `tests/configuration.rs::explicit_credentials_round_trip_but_diagnostics_and_debug_redact_them` checks each rejection.\n\n**Passing credentials to the provider**\n- `bedrock_sdk_config` (`src/authoring/providers.rs`, lines 109-138) passes explicit values straight to the AWS loader through `credentials_provider(...)`. It does not touch process environment variables or write files.\n- The Ollama path in `RigProvider::connect` passes the key with `with_api_key`.\n- `explicit_bedrock_credentials_reach_the_sdk_without_the_default_chain` checks the Bedrock path.\n- The CLI test in `tests/authoring_cli.rs` checks that the Ollama key reaches the request headers.\n\n**Serialization**\n- `SecretString` serializes transparently, so saved values round-trip. The round-trip assertion is in the `explicit_credentials_round_trip...` test.\n- `api_key_from` resolves environment values at runtime only. `ollama_authentication_resolves_separately_and_rejects_missing_values` asserts that the serialized form keeps `MODEL_KEY` and never contains the resolved value.\n- `ResolvedAuthoringConfig` is documented as holding no resolved secrets.\n\n**Redaction**\n- `SecretString`'s `Debug` prints `<redacted>`, and the Debug assertions in the round-trip test confirm it.\n- `parse_document` reports only the line and column of a TOML syntax error, never source text.\n- `decode` calls `set_input(None)` and replaces every value found under `credentials` or `api_key`. This includes integer values and quoted forms.\n- The test covers these cases: array and trailing-garbage `api_key`, a string where the `credentials` table belongs, and an integer `api_key`. It runs them through both `WorkspaceConfig::parse` and `UserConfig::parse`.\n- Usage records hold no configuration values. `authoring_uses_nearest_workspace_configuration_without_a_user_file` checks that the literal key is absent from the request body (which carries the prompt), the usage JSON, stdout and stderr, while still appearing in the auth header.\n\n**Source-read exclusion**\n- `load_workspace_evidence` sets `contains_credentials` when any `authoring.configs.*` table has a `credentials` or `api_key` key. It does this even if the rest of the file is invalid.\n- `PathPolicy::load` and `contains_credentials` match the requested path, the resolved target and the canonicalized config path. That covers symlink aliases.\n- `SourceAccess::path` denies the file after the disallow and allow checks. An explicit `allow` therefore still overrides the exclusion, as the requirement says.\n- `list_files`, `ripgrep` and the other tools reach it through `access.path` and `access.file`, so they all apply it.\n- `workspace_credentials_are_excluded_unless_explicitly_allowed` checks denial, that the error text omits the key, and that an explicit allow works.\n- `evidence_configuration_ignores_unused_authoring_settings_and_detects_credentials` checks detection and that errors omit the credential text.\n- The tool descriptions in `src/authoring/tools.rs` state the default exclusion.\n\n**Documentation**\n- `md/requirements/authoring.md` (the \"Editing the file\" section) says literal credentials are supported but not recommended, and that a workspace file may be committed. It recommends profiles and `api_key_env`.\n- Its example uses placeholders such as `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `OLLAMA_API_KEY_VALUE`.\n- It explains that an explicit allow exposes the file to the model and records its contents as evidence inputs.\n- `md/reference.md` repeats the guidance and carries the `{cite}` for this requirement.\n\n**Gaps**\n- No test directly asserts that the process environment is unchanged or that no credential file is written. That is inherent in the straightforward code path, so the absence is acceptable.\n- No test confirms usage records are free of Bedrock credentials specifically. The records contain no configuration values at all, and the Ollama test checks the same record structure.\n- Beyond the `{impl}` annotations, no behavior is left unmarked.\n- No FIXMEs are present.",
